# WebRTC and signaling security

- Validate every WebSocket payload at runtime and cap signaling messages at 64 KiB.
- Generate room codes with cryptographic randomness, limit rooms to two peers, and delete empty rooms.
- Relay only SDP descriptions and ICE candidates; never log or route chat text or file bytes.
- Check WebSocket origins in production and use `wss:` whenever the page uses HTTPS.
- Queue ICE candidates until the remote description exists, and close every channel/listener on leave.
- Keep file transfers bounded, chunked, backpressured, and initiated only after explicit user selection.
- WebRTC data channels use DTLS encryption, but do not claim verified peer identity without fingerprint verification.
- Never commit static TURN credentials. Prefer short-lived TURN credentials issued by a trusted service.
